is perhaps the toughest sanction the  west has levied against russia around 70  of its banks and financial institutions  have been cut off from swift the system  used by banks to send money across  borders it is a move that's impact  reaches well beyond russia itself many  banks in the u.s the eu and the uk have  given loans to russian firms who can now  no longer service them it's unclear  exactly how much banks have loaned to  russian companies but the experts are  putting this number somewhere in the  tens of billions of euros then there is  of course the potential for sovereign  debt default russia's central bank can't  access its assets abroad and is on  course to miss repayments to its  creditors now russia currently owes  nearly 50 billion dollars to lenders in  the west payments are due within the  next two weeks one for 100 million  dollars in interest another of over 2  billion  among the countries levying sanctions on  russia is switzerland it's an  unprecedented step for the financial hub  that prides itself on staying neutral  but it's still being urged to take  further action  zurich is one of the world's most  expensive places and that's in no small  part due to russian oligarchs happy to  invest their money here  rudolph elma used to be a top banker at  the venerable zurich bank yulia spare  today he is a critic of these financial  institutions who came to international  prominence as a wikileaks whistleblower  in this rightful field  a lot of money is invested in  switzerland because banking secrecy  protects the oligarchs we have a strict  secrecy law  the government politicians and the  judiciary support this because it is a  huge source of income for the swiss  state  russia's brutal war against ukraine has  put worldwide pressure on switzerland to  respond it's likely one of the reasons  why the government here has backed the  sanctions saying russian oligarchs  should no longer be able to access their  assets here but elmer is skeptical  switzerland was slow to introduce  sanctions and that gave the oligarchs  plenty of time to shift their fortune  somewhere else  concealing the origin of money is a long  tradition in switzerland  this means that the actual owner is  disguised via trusts in the cayman  islands or companies or whatever in such  a way that you don't really know who's  behind  it the swiss bankers association rejects  this accusation saying the sanctions  would be strictly observed  it's important to understand that these  sanctions oblige banks to report russian  customers to the authorities banking  secrecy is never absolute  it can be affected by a legal obligation  to provide information and that is also  the case with these sanctions  this is concrete  sanctions  gazprom bank and spare bank seen here in  zurich are exempt from the sanctions  that allows eu countries like germany to  still be able to buy russian oil and gas  through them but public criticism is  growing spare bank in zurich has covered  over its logo and lowered window blinds  for fear of protests  the swiss ngo public eye has long  criticized the russian banks and now it  says they're playing a critical role in  moscow's war  this is of course a substantial part of  vladimir putin's war chest he calculated  it that way from the start  and also anticipated that this money at  least would continue to flow  we're talking about a billion dollars a  day eighty percent of which comes from  swiss commodities trading platforms  one of the data centers for the swift  bank payment system is very close to  zurich it's currently under heavy guard  for fear of russian sabotage but elmer  doesn't think a complete russian cutoff  from swift would be that dramatic for  russia  there are various ways of getting around  this like inter-company deals  banks holding bank accounts at other  banks  and trade shortcuts as well as barter  transactions  there are many variants and you have to  see that this payment transaction  business has become even more lucrative  today due to the sanctions  rudolf elmer now lives in seclusion near  zurich but he hasn't stopped criticizing  switzerland's financial institutions and  authorities he's demanding strict laws  for better monitoring of the financial  centre but so far says he's seen no  willingness in the government to take  action  well i've been speaking to jurg rockhole  who's president of the european school  of management and technology esmt berlin  i asked him how surprising it was that  switzerland had joined the eu in  sanctioning russia  the  fact that switzerland joined is  certainly just representing the overall  observation that we have at this point  of time  that uh there is such a strong  unified approach across western  countries that no one could escape and  in particular we've seen in the past  that any bank that tries to escape  or say circumvent sanctions has been  penalized heavily by in particular u.s  authorities so therefore it might be a  little bit surprising but in light of  the overall development and also in  light of what we've seen as a penalties  in the past it's maybe not that  surprising  is it a big blow to the russian banking  system to have these sanctions from  switzerland in particular given the role  that it it plays with its much stricter  secrecy laws  this is certainly a blow to russian  banks but maybe it's even more a blow  in the asset management part so this  means  if wealthy russians  put their money to switzerland now um  they are sanctioned as well swiss banks  are extremely careful in now um  operating on the level uh actually with  any russian client and thus it becomes  also more difficult to withdraw private  money from switzerland and use it for  other purposes  is this the sort of change that  could last for a long time what what's  what's the feeling with these sanctions  that they're going to be in place  forever and russians are permanently  going to have to find a new way to move  their money around and indeed move their  their exports  um i see two elements here for sure i  would expect the sanctions to be in  place for the foreseeable future  but there's another aspect and maybe  this aspect might become even more  important so in the long run which is  the voting by feed mechanism that we see  now we have seen that many western  companies even companies that were not  restricted to do so by the sanctions  that they have actually started  withdrawing their business from russia  that also western companies don't want  to work anymore which would say russian  subsidiaries in europe in the united  states and this voting by feed has taken  really a very massive a very strong  movement over the last two weeks and  this is something that in my view has  not seen the end yet but rather will  continue and then it will be very hard  to revert these decisions in particular  as we see now if the russian government  even wants to nationalize to expropriate  the assets that western companies have  left in russia  yeah it's a major change for russia that  keeps  getting worse it keeps getting more and  more isolated from the rest of the world  and  indeed  it's looking less and less likely that  russia's going to be able to pay its  debts to its foreign creditors default  looks or almost certain doesn't it  yes indeed the rating agencies have now  made very clear  statements that russian sovereign debt  just is at a junk status  it's also not clear anymore whether even  if russia did have the money  whether they would be even willing to  pay so that they basically voluntarily  declare default this is why  basically  even more of the linkages of the  international financial systems to  russia will be stopped  and it will even isolate russia more in  many different ways and the financial  system is certainly at the forefront of  this first wave of sanctions  you
 

